   6: # `/work` â€” the one verb
   7: 
   8: `/work` Â· `/work AI4DEV-19` Â· `/work AI4PM-12` Â· `/work explore`
   9: 
  10: There is one lifecycle and one entry point. Everything below is a phase inside this skill,
  11: never a second verb.
  12: 
  13: ## The rule that shapes everything
  14: 
  15: Every attribution failure in this project was one shape: a **declared** fact drifted from a
  16: **real** fact, and nothing could detect the gap. The system is allowed to say "I don't know".
  17: It is never allowed to be confidently wrong.
  18: 
  19: So: attribution is **derived from the branch**, never declared. The branch is coupled to
  20: closure â€” its pull request closes that item â€” so a wrong branch shows up as a wrong closure
  21: on the board instead of hiding.
  22: 
  23: ```
  24: branch and held item disagree -> CONFLICT. Show both, ask. Never pick one.
  25: branch names one item         -> that item; walk parents for the chain
  26: branch names 2+               -> unresolved
  27: branch names none, session HOLDS one -> that, marked held-not-branch
  28: explicitly exploring          -> exploration
  29: otherwise                     -> unattributed
  30: ```
  31: 
  32: The **held item is a cross-check, never an answer**: it can raise CONFLICT or fill a gap the
  33: branch left empty, but it can never override the branch. A stale held item makes the stamp
  34: louder, never wronger â€” the exact inverse of the binding file this replaces.
  35: 
  36: **Attribution never blocks building.** Linear unreachable, chain ambiguous, root missing: say
  37: so and carry on. The one thing it blocks is **closing a requirement**, because that is where a
  38: wrong answer becomes permanent.
  39: 
  40: ## The agent never moves itself (founder 2026-08-02)
  41: 
  42: A session works in the folder it was launched in, on one branch, for the whole item. It may
  43: create a worktree and *tell the founder to open a session there*; it never teleports into one.
  44: 
  45: - **Serial work needs no worktree**: switch the branch in the folder you are in.
  46: - **A worktree is only for a second item at the same time**, and the founder opens that session.
  47: - Never rely on a tool that moves the session. If such a move silently failed, the session
  48:   would sit in `main` believing it was elsewhere â€” which is exactly the AI4DEV-24 failure.
  49: 
  50: ## Phase A â€” decide what
  51: 
  52: | you type | what happens |
  53: |---|---|
  54: | `/work AI4DEV-19` | a LEAF â€” build it (phase B) |
  55: | `/work AI4DEV-3` | a PARENT â€” list its open children, recommend one, **wait** |
  56: | `/work AI4PM-12` | requirement â€” five states below |
  57: | `/work explore` | declare deliberately untracked work |
  58: | `/work` | **recommend and wait** |
  59: 
  60: **A dev item with children is a container, not work** (founder question 2026-08-03: *"if I run
  61: work on AI4DEV-3, what does it do for its children?"*). Check for children BEFORE treating any
  62: id as buildable. A parent has no code of its own, so cutting a branch for it would produce a
  63: pull request with nothing in it and an item that can never close on its own merge â€” parents
  64: close by **folding** when their last child closes, never by being built.
  65: 
  66: So a parent behaves exactly like a claimed requirement: list the open children with their short
  67: labels, say which are blocked and by what, recommend one with a reason, and wait. The founder
  68: picks. Closed children are named in the listing too, because "5 of 13 done" is the useful frame
  69: and a bare list of what remains hides it.
  70: 
  71: **`/work` alone** â€” never picks silently. In order: resume what this session holds; else
  72: anything already In Progress; else open leaves under a claimed requirement; else a new
  73: requirement to claim, with its decomposition state named. Show the top three with short
  74: labels and a one-line reason each, recommend one, **wait**.
  75: 
  76: **`/work AI4PM-12`** reports which state it found and proposes; it never guesses which you meant.
  77: 
  78: | state | action |
  79: |---|---|
  80: | no decomposition file | propose writing `loop/decomp/req-0NN.md` as the work â€” its own branch, through this loop |
  81: | decomposition merged, unclaimed | claim it, **materialize the dev tree**, list leaves, recommend one, wait |
  82: | claimed, leaves open | list open leaves, recommend one, wait |
  83: | all leaves closed | run the evidence gate, propose closing |
  84: | Done | say so, offer what is next |
  85: 
  86: **Materialization** creates the dev root as a **sub-issue of the requirement**
  87: (`parentId = AI4PM-12`) â€” that parent link is what makes the chain walk work. It reads the
  88: decomposition at a merged commit, is idempotent (matched by exact title), and when scope
  89: changes it adds new leaves but **never removes a leaf that has work against it**.
  90: 
  91: ## Phase B â€” prepare the workspace
  92: 
  93: Read-only validation first. **The board claim happens last**, after everything else succeeded,
  94: so a failure can never leave an item falsely In Progress.
  95: 
  96: 1. Resolve the item: id, short label, `gitBranchName`, state, blockers; walk `parent` upward
  97:    (depth cap 8, cycle detection) to the root; derive short labels for every id in the chain.
  98: 
  99:    **Deriving a short label: strip the internal code, keep the meaning.** Board titles often
 100:    lead with one â€” `H5 â€” `, `REQ-0NN â€” `, `Batch 3 â€” `. Truncating from the front preserves the
 101:    code and discards what the item actually is, which is backwards. `AI4DEV-21 (fake Stripe,
 102:    GitHub, Anthropic)`, never `AI4DEV-21 (H5 vendor stand-ins)`. Two to five words a stranger
 103:    could act on.
 104: 2. Startability **blocks**: item missing, Done, Cancelled, or an open blocker â†’ stop and say
 105:    which. Attribution failures do **not** block â€” they print and continue.
 106: 2a. **If the walk finds no requirement above the item, ASK â€” once, here at pickup**
 107:    (founder ruling 2026-08-02). Two failures that look alike must not be treated alike:
 108: 
 109:    | what happened | what to do |
 110:    |---|---|
 111:    | the walk **completed** and the item has no parent | **stop and ask.** The board is saying nothing is above this, which is a 
modelling gap and the founder is the one who can close it. |
 112:    | the board could **not be read** (API error, unreachable, partial response) | **do not ask.** That is a technical failure, not a 
modelling gap â€” asking would bake a guess into the board permanently. Print `CHAIN UNRESOLVED`, carry on, retry at the next boundary. |
 113: 
 114:    When asking, **offer suggestions rather than an open question**, ranked:
 115:    1. dev-board items that already have children â€” the real phases (e.g. `AI4DEV-3 (AT harness)`,
 116:       `AI4DEV-4 (the work skill)`), nearest first by project and subject;
 117:    2. an `AI4PM` requirement, when the item's text points at one;
 118:    3. **a free-text grouping label** â€” see floating roots below;
 119:    4. **"standalone â€” it is its own root"**, always offered and always legitimate. Not every
 120:       item belongs to a phase, and a phase with exactly one child tells you less than no phase.
 121: 
 122:    Then record the answer so it is asked **once, ever**:
 123:    - a parent was chosen â†’ `save_issue` sets `parentId`; the chain resolves permanently, for
 124:      every future session, because the answer lives on the board rather than on this machine;
 125:    - free text â†’ a FLOATING root, below;
 126:    - standalone â†’ label the item `standalone-root`, which is why the question does not return;
 127:    - **no answer, or the founder declines â†’ proceed with no root and do not ask again this
 128:      session.** Attribution degrades and never blocks; this is a question at the one moment a
 129:      human is present, not a gate.
 130: 
 131:    The stamp hook never asks anything â€” it runs before every prompt and blocking there is not
 132:    an option. This is `/work` at pickup, which is a deliberate interactive moment.
 133: 
 134: ### Floating roots (founder ruling 2026-08-02)
 135: 
 136: A free-text answer is **accepted as-is**. It becomes a floating root â€” a grouping that exists
 137: only for the attribution log and has **no item on the board**. The founder's reason, and it
 138: governs the design: *the point of attribution is a log we can chew on for cadence and
 139: monitoring*, and a grouping useful for that does not need a board item behind it.
 140: 
 141: I argued for creating a real item instead; the founder overruled, and the ruling stands. What
 142: the implementation must therefore guarantee is that a floating root is never mistaken for a
 143: verified one:
 144: 
 145: - **Written `~name`** â€” e.g. `~infrastructure`. The tilde makes it unmistakable to a reader and
 146:   to a parser, so an analysis of the log can always separate "grouped by a real requirement"
 147:   from "grouped by a label someone typed".
 148: - **Legal only as the ROOT of a chain.** Anywhere else it would be asserting a board
 149:   relationship that does not exist.
 150: - **A real parent always wins.** If the walk later finds an actual parent, that is the chain and
 151:   the floating label is dropped with a note. The traversal catches genuine structure; a floating
 152:   root only fills space the board leaves empty.
 153: - **Recorded as a Linear label** (`attr:<name>`) on the item as well as in the cache, so it
 154:   survives a machine and stays queryable for the monitoring it exists to serve â€” a label is a
 155:   tag on the item, not a parent, so this creates nothing on the PM board.
 156: 
 157: ```
 158: WORKING ON  ~infrastructure > AI4DEV-5 (CI pipeline)
 159: IN          wt ai4good - branch nirdrang/ai4dev-5-bring-up-ci-pipeline-...
 160: ```
 161: 3. **Branch name comes from Linear's `gitBranchName` verbatim** â€” never invented. That is what
 162:    makes the pull request close the right item. Validate it tokenises to exactly this item.
 163: 4. Put the folder on that branch. Serial work: switch this folder. A second concurrent item:
 164:    create a worktree, then **ask the founder to open a session there** â€” do not proceed here.
 165: 5. Claim: assign, In Progress.
 166: 6. **Print the transition record** the moment the branch changes â€” the pre-prompt stamp
 167:    describes the turn as it began, so a turn that changes branch must say so:
 168: 
 169: ```
 170: TRANSITION  AI4PM-19 (user auth) > AI4DEV-41 (login form)
 171:             branch nirdrang/ai4dev-41-login-form  base 8c3a5ca
 172: ```
 173: 
 174: 7. Journal each step so a crash between any two is recoverable by reading, not guessing.
 175: 
 176: ## Running items in PARALLEL â€” one agent per item (tested 2026-08-02)
 177: 
 178: Serial work needs none of this: one item, worked on a branch in the folder you are already in.
 179: This is for running two or three items at once **without the founder opening a session for
 180: each**.
 181: 
 182: **The shape.** The coordinator session spawns ONE agent per item with worktree isolation. The
 183: platform creates that agent's worktree and **the agent is born inside it** â€” it never has to
 184: move itself, which is what made every earlier attempt fail. It is also *pinned*: the platform
 185: refuses any command that resolves outside its worktree, so it cannot damage another item's
 186: work even by mistake.
 187: 
 188: ```
 189: coordinator (main checkout, never moves) - spawns, and merges. Nothing else.
 190: â””â”€â”€ item agent - FABLE - own worktree, own branch, FULL AUTHORITY over its item
 191:     â”œâ”€â”€ executor          - OPUS - writes the code, same worktree, same branch
 192:     â”œâ”€â”€ mechanical        - sonnet - housekeeping, publish, merge, courier
 193:     â”œâ”€â”€ gate reviewers    - codex TERRA @ max + Kimi k3 @ high, in the worktree
 194:     â””â”€â”€ pre-merge auditor - codex LUNA @ max, workspace-write, in the worktree
 195: ```
 196: 
 197: Terra reviews the code; luna audits the claim about it. A different vendor from the agent that
 198: wrote the code, and a different variant from the one that reviewed it.
 199: 
 200: Spawned as: `Agent(subagent_type: "item-agent", isolation: "worktree",
 201: run_in_background: true, model: "fable", prompt: <the item brief>)`. The `isolation` line is
 202: what creates the worktree; nothing else creates one.
 203: 
 204: **Effort comes from the agent DEFINITION, not the call.** The Agent tool sets `model` but has
 205: no effort parameter, so "opus at max effort" cannot be requested at the call site.
 206: `.claude/agents/item-agent.md` carries `effort: max` in its frontmatter, and effort is not a
 207: caller parameter â€” so it applies whichever model the caller picks. To vary effort per call,
 208: define another agent type; the choice lives in `subagent_type`.
 209: 
 210: That file also carries the standing role â€” no code, derive your own chain, push at every phase
 211: boundary, how to ask a question â€” so a brief carries only what is specific to its item. A
 212: shorter brief is a brief with fewer places to be wrong.
 213: 
 214: ### A brief states what to RESOLVE, never a resolved value
 215: 
 216: The coordinator must not put a derived fact into a brief. Not the chain, not the parent, not a
 217: label, not a state the agent can read for itself. Say *"resolve your chain from the board"*,
 218: never *"your chain is X > Y"*.
 219: 
 220: This was violated on AI4DEV-31: the brief contained a literal `Set-Chain â€¦ @{id='AI4DEV-3'â€¦}`.
 221: It happened to be right, and that is the danger â€” the agent would have stamped a wrong value
 222: just as faithfully, for the life of the item, and no gate reads briefs. It is the
 223: declared-not-derived defect this entire way of work exists to delete, reintroduced by the one
 224: actor nobody reviews.
 225: 
 226: Two nets already exist and neither is a substitute for the rule: the agent definition tells the
 227: agent to treat a handed chain as a hint to verify, and Gate 0 is asked to flag "a fact asserted
 228: that the agent could derive itself". Both fire AFTER the brief is written. This one fires
 229: before.
 230: 
 231: **The general form, because it is what makes any of this survive:** a correction exists only if
 232: it lives in a file that loads every session â€” the skill, the agent definition, CLAUDE.md,
 233: memory. A correction delivered in a message to a running agent dies with that agent. If it
 234: matters for the next item, write it down before the conversation moves on.
 235: 
 236: **`--fallback-model` does not rescue this.** It fires on model overload and non-retryable server
 237: errors; authentication, **billing**, rate-limit, request-size and transport errors explicitly
 238: never trigger it. Out of credits is a billing error, so the fableâ†’opus handoff stays manual and
 239: must be **stated in the report** when it happens.
 240: 
 241: **The item agent has FULL AUTHORITY over its item and never sends judgment back to the
 242: coordinator** (founder ruling 2026-08-02: *"why does the item agent need judgment back to you â€”
 243: these are free agents"*). It triages findings, rules on them, and decides what merges. An
 244: earlier draft had it escalating to the coordinator, which would have made the parallelism fake:
 245: three agents all queued behind one conversation, with the coordinator as the serializer.
 246: 
 247: What still leaves the item goes **to the founder directly, not through the coordinator** â€” a
 248: finding that contradicts ratified text, scope growth, a disputed false-green tag. The
 249: coordinator spawns and merges. That is the whole of its job.
 250: 
 251: Its subagents **inherit its worktree and branch** (tested: a child reported the identical
 252: directory and branch, and read a file its parent had just written). The coordinator does not
 253: review the work in place â€” it reads what the agent **published**, the pushed branch and the PR,
 254: which is the same surface the founder reviews.
 255: 
 256: ### Models (founder ruling 2026-08-02)
 257: 
 258: | role | model | what it does |
 259: |---|---|---|
 260: | **item agent â€” the orchestrator** | **fable** (â†’ opus only when fable is out of credit) | **judgment only. Writes no code.** 
Brief, checkpoint, rulings, merge decision |
 261: | **executor** | **opus** | **writes the code.** Plans, implements, triages findings first-hand, fixes |
 262: | mechanical | **sonnet** | housekeeping, publish, merge execution, courier runs |
 263: | **pre-merge auditor** | codex `gpt-5.6-luna` @ `max` | independent re-run â€” see below |
 264: | gate reviewers | codex `gpt-5.6-terra` @ `max`, Kimi `k3` @ `high` | adversarial critique |
 265: 
 266: **Three tiers, and the boundary is strict** (founder ruling 2026-08-03: *"fable as orchestrator
 267: is doing judgments, not creating code â€” that's the opus executor role"*). Premium credits buy
 268: decisions, not keystrokes. The orchestrator writes the brief with every decision pre-made, rules
 269: on findings, and signs the merge decision; **it does not implement.** An earlier draft had it
 270: driving the work itself and handing edits to sonnet â€” that spent fable tokens on typing and put
 271: judgment and implementation in one context, where a decision can be quietly revised by whoever
 272: is doing the typing.
 273: 
 274: The executor escalates judgment **to its own item agent**, not to the coordinator â€” that is
 275: inside the item and costs no parallelism. When the fable fallback is used, **say so in the
 276: report**, because a fable item run and an opus item run are not the same evidence.
 277: 
 278: ### The auditor is codex luna (founder ruling 2026-08-03)
 279: 
 280: It runs before merge and independently re-runs the verification, gathering each checklist box's
 281: evidence â€” because **the agent claiming green is the one being checked**, and its own transcript
 282: is contaminated by having written the code.
 283: 
 284: **Independence is the property being bought.** A fresh in-house context gives one kind; a
 285: different vendor gives a stronger one. And **luna audits while terra reviews**, so the model
 286: that examined the code is not the model auditing the claim about it â€” the two variants'
 287: blind spots are decorrelated rather than shared.
 288: 
 289: ```
 290: codex exec --sandbox workspace-write -C <worktree> \
 291:   -c model=gpt-5.6-luna -c model_reasoning_effort=max \
 292:   -o <worktree>/loop/items/<ITEM>/premerge-audit.md "<audit brief>"
 293: ```
 294: 
 295: **`--sandbox workspace-write` is load-bearing, not incidental.** The auditor must actually RUN
 296: the suite; under `read-only` the test run fails and the audit silently degrades into a
 297: documentation review that reports green because it never executed anything. That failure would
 298: look exactly like a passing audit.
 299: 
 300: Run it **detached** â€” a max-effort pass that also executes a test suite will exceed the
 301: ten-minute foreground ceiling.
 302: 
 303: **It reports; it rules on nothing.** The ruling belongs to the item agent, which holds authority
 304: over its item. If codex is unavailable, fall back to a fresh-context **sonnet** auditor and say
 305: which was used â€” an audit that is quietly skipped is worse than one that is openly weaker.
 306: 
 307: **Known boundary (2026-08-03): in a PLATFORM worktree â€” always nested inside the main
 308: checkout â€” the workspace-scoped sandbox denies vitest's ancestor-directory config walk**
 309: (`Cannot read directory "../../../../.."`), so vitest-based boxes fail with access-denied
 310: while non-walking commands pass. Read that signature as COULD-NOT-VERIFY-IN-SANDBOX, take the
 311: execution evidence from the PR's own CI run instead, and say so â€” it is not a red of the code
 312: under audit.
 313: 
 314: ### Rules the testing forced
 315: 
 316: - **Liveness of a detached reviewer is judged by ITS OWN ARTIFACTS, never by process scans
 317:   or someone else's observation** (added 2026-08-03, after two wrong death calls on one
 318:   item). An unflushed output file reads as zero bytes while the reviewer is mid-run; kimi
 319:   runs as a node process, so a name scan misses it. The owning agent checks the task
 320:   transcript's size and last-write time across a short interval â€” growth means alive. Only
 321:   a verified-EMPTY result after completion is a failed gate: one relaunch, then rule the
 322:   gate unavailable in writing and proceed. A coordinator does not make liveness claims; it
 323:   points at files and lets the owner measure.
 324: - **NEVER resume an agent after it has finished.** Its worktree is deleted on completion, and a
 325:   resumed agent silently falls back to the main checkout. Observed: a resumed probe created a
 326:   branch in the live checkout and switched it. If an agent must wait for a ruling, it stays
 327:   **alive** â€” a live agent keeps its worktree.
 328: - **Push before finishing.** The worktree dies with the agent, so anything uncommitted is lost.
 329:   The remote is the only durable output.
 330: - **Never hand an agent a worktree you created.** It cannot use one; the isolation guard blocks
 331:   it and bricks the agent's shell (tested â€” `EnterWorktree` reported success and every
 332:   subsequent command was refused). Worktrees are the platform's to create.
 333: 
 334: The auto-generated folder name (`agent-<hash>`) is irrelevant, because **attribution reads the
 335: branch, not the folder**. The agent checks out the item's Linear branch inside its own worktree
 336: and the stamp resolves correctly.
 337: 
 338: ### Footgun
 339: 
 340: **PowerShell keeps no shell state between tool calls** â€” only the working directory persists.
 341: A script that dot-sources `work-lib.ps1` in one call and uses its functions in the next will
 342: fail with "not recognized". Source and use in the *same* command.
 343: 
 344: ## Phase C â€” build
 345: 
 346: **Gate 0** (sol refutes the BRIEF) â†’ plan â†’ **Gate 1** (terra refutes the plan) â†’ triage â†’
 347: orchestrator checkpoint â†’ implement â†’ **Gate 2** (terra + Kimi in parallel on the diff) â†’
 348: bounded fix cycles â†’ verify.
 349: 
 350: ### Gate 0 â€” the brief itself is reviewed (founder ruling 2026-08-03)
 351: 
 352: **codex `-c model=gpt-5.6-sol -c model_reasoning_effort=max`**, in the worktree, detached.
 353: 
 354: The brief is where every decision is pre-made, so it is the highest-leverage document in an
 355: item â€” and until now it was the only one nobody read adversarially. Gate 1 reviews a plan and
 356: Gate 2 reviews a diff; both check implementation against stated intent, so **neither can catch
 357: a defect in the intent**. Three real defects reached the founder on 2026-08-03 for exactly that
 358: reason, one of them a coordinator hardcoding a derived fact into a brief where no gate would
 359: ever see it.
 360: 
 361: Sol reviews briefs and designs, terra reviews code, luna audits the claim. Three variants,
 362: three jobs, decorrelated blind spots.
 363: 
 364: Ask it specifically for what a plan review cannot surface: a decision stated as settled that is
 365: not actually decided; a fact asserted that the agent could derive itself; a constraint that
 366: contradicts the skill; scope that will force a mid-flight redesign; and anything the brief
 367: requires that no tool can do. **A brief that cannot be executed as written is the failure this
 368: gate exists to catch** â€” an executor blocked mid-item is expensive, and the block was knowable
 369: before a line was written.
 370: 
 371: **Proportionality (founder 2026-08-02).** The gates exist for correctness risk. An item that
 372: is documentation, or whose design has already been through adversarial review, runs a single
 373: focused gate on the part that is actually code â€” and says in the report that it did. Running
 374: nine phases of ceremony on a prose change is how a process stops being followed at all.
 375: 
 376: **Run gates in the BACKGROUND.** A max-effort review of a real diff exceeds the ten-minute
 377: foreground command ceiling and gets killed â€” observed twice on 2026-08-02, losing the whole
 378: run both times. Launch the reviewer detached, keep working, fold the findings when it returns.
 379: 
 380: **Reviewers run IN THE WORKTREE, against the real tree â€” never against an exported diff**
 381: (2026-08-02, after the founder pushed back on this). A reviewer is a process with a working
 382: directory, so `codex -C <worktree>` reads the actual source: it can follow a call into a file
 383: the diff never touched. Exporting a diff to a scratchpad and reviewing that was a habit
 384: mistaken for a limitation, and it made every gate weaker than it needed to be â€” the reviewer
 385: could only see the lines it was handed. **Proven**: codex launched in a worktree read
 386: `stamp-hook.ps1` unprompted and quoted its literal strings back.
 387: 
 388: **Reviewer sessions are RESUMED for confirmation, never rebuilt** (founder 2026-08-02: coming
 389: back to a fresh session "recreates the context it built an iteration ago â€” complete waste").
 390: It is worse than waste: a new session re-derives its findings from scratch, so the rule that
 391: *a finding is confirmed by the reviewer that raised it* is quietly broken â€” a different chain
 392: of reasoning is answering for the first one. Capture the session id from the gate run and
 393: resume it:
 394: - codex: `codex exec resume <SESSION_ID> "<confirmation prompt>"`
 395: - Kimi: `kimi -S <id>`, or `-c` to continue the previous session **for this working
 396:   directory** â€” which scopes to the item's worktree by itself
 397: 
 398: Reviewer pins, in each vendor's own vocabulary â€” **the ladders differ, and invalid values fall
 399: back silently**:
 400: - codex: `-c model=gpt-5.6-terra -c model_reasoning_effort=max` (ladder tops at `max`; `xhigh`
 401:   is one tier below â€” believing otherwise already cost a real under-run)
 402: - Kimi: `kimi -m kimi-code/k3 -p "<short>" --output-format text`, effort `high` from config
 403: 
 404: Useful codex flags, all verified present: `-C <dir>` sets the working root, `-o <file>` writes
 405: the final answer to a file instead of leaving it to be scraped from stdout, and `--json` emits
 406: events as JSONL.
 407: 
 408: ### How to actually launch a reviewer â€” this cost three failed launches on AI4DEV-5
 409: 
 410: Written down because it was folklore, and folklore is rediscovered by failing:
 411: 
 412: - **Short prompt on the command line, brief in a FILE.** The reviewer runs in the worktree and
 413:   can read files, so pass a pointer â€” "read `loop/items/<ITEM>/gate2-prompt.txt` and the diff"
 414:   â€” not the material itself. A long inline prompt hits the Windows argument limit and dies, and
 415:   embedded quotes mangle it before that.
 416: - **Capture with `-o <file>`**, never by scraping stdout. Redirect **stderr into the same file**
 417:   too: a failure message in the file teaches you why it died; a silent 71-byte file teaches you
 418:   nothing (observed).
 419: - **`codex exec resume <SESSION_ID>` rejects `-C`.** Resume inherits the original session's
 420:   working root; passing the flag again fails the invocation.
 421: - **Kimi: `-p` is incompatible with `--auto` and `--yolo`.** The working form is
 422:   `kimi -m kimi-code/k3 -p "<short>" --output-format text`.
 423: - **Size is not the test â€” CONTENT is.** A 71-byte file containing
 424:   `"â€¢ Reading the brief's context filesâ€¦"` is a progress line, not a critique. Check for
 425:   findings, and check the exit code. "Non-empty" was too weak a rule and let a failed gate look
 426:   like a clean one.
 427: - **Never judge a reviewer's liveness from a process list** (the coordinator did, twice, and was
 428:   wrong both times â€” once from an unflushed file, once from a scan that did not match how Kimi
 429:   runs). Measure the reviewer's own artifacts growing over an interval. The owner of a detached
 430:   process is the only party that can judge whether it is alive.
 431: - **Watch the SESSION TRANSCRIPT, not the `-o` file.** `-o` writes only the final answer, and
 432:   the redirected streams can stay empty for several minutes, so a healthy reviewer looks dead
 433:   for its whole opening stretch â€” an item agent nearly killed one on exactly this. The
 434:   reviewer's own transcript grows continuously and is the honest liveness signal.
 435: 
 436: ### ONE WRITER IN A WORKTREE AT A TIME
 437: 
 438: The skill told item agents to run reviewers in their worktree, and separately to use subagents
 439: freely, and never said those must not overlap. One agent lost **two** audit runs to this: a
 440: mutating subagent stashed the `workspace-write` auditor's in-flight changes, and later a kill
 441: hit a wrapper while the real process survived and kept writing into the tree for eight more
 442: minutes.
 443: 
 444: So: **only one writer touches a worktree at a time.** While a `workspace-write` reviewer or
 445: auditor is running, nothing else mutates that tree â€” no editing subagent, no commit, no stash,
 446: no checkout. And a reviewer's process must be **confirmed dead by process id**, not by its
 447: wrapper exiting and not by an empty output file, before anything else writes there. A wrapper
 448: that has returned is not evidence that the process it launched has stopped.
 449: 
 450: Confirmation runs at `high`; a confirmation asked to judge a *claim* rather than a fix is
 451: review work and gets `max`.
 452: 
 453: ### A ruling that REMOVES work carries a verification condition
 454: 
 455: When you accept a gate finding that says *"this part is unnecessary, take it out"*, attach a
 456: condition the executor must check before removing it â€” and restore the work if the condition
 457: fails.
 458: 
 459: This is not caution for its own sake. An item accepted a Gate 1 finding that converting one
 460: type bought nothing; the measurement behind it covered only the direct read and missed an
 461: upcast route, so the ruling was wrong. **Kimi caught it at Gate 2 â€” a finding terra missed, and
 462: what it caught was an error in the item agent's own earlier ruling.** It was recoverable only
 463: because the removal had carried a condition to verify.
 464: 
 465: Removals are the rulings least likely to be re-examined: nothing downstream fails, the diff
 466: gets smaller, and the reviewer that proposed it has already moved on. A condition is the only
 467: thing that makes a removal falsifiable later.
 468: 
 469: ## Committing â€” `.claude/` and `loop/out/` are GITIGNORED
 470: 
 471: Both are ignored by directory rule and their contents are tracked only because they were
 472: force-added. **A new file under either path will not stage, and `git add -A` reports nothing
 473: wrong.** So: `git add -f <path>`, then **read `git status --short` and confirm the `A` line is
 474: there** before committing.
 475: 
 476: Caught on this item as a near-miss: the commit was about to delete eight verb skills and add
 477: zero replacements, because the new skill silently did not stage. Verify the add; do not trust it.
 478: 
 479: ## Phase D â€” finish
 480: 
 481: A completion signal is always something **outside the agent's own claim**.
 482: 
 483: | level | signal | not the signal |
 484: |---|---|---|
 485: | leaf | PR merged **and** Linear flipped it Done | "the tests passed for me" |
 486: | parent | every child closed, re-read fresh | memory of what was closed |
 487: | requirement | the evidence gate | its leaves being closed |
 488: 
 489: 1. Independent re-verification by a fresh-context agent â€” never the one that wrote the code.
 490: 2. Written merge ruling, pinned to the head commit.
 491: 3. PR published with that ruling; merge authorization pinned to the same commit.
 492: 4. Merge â†’ the integration flips the item Done. See "when git and the board disagree" below.
 493: 
 494: **INTERIM MODE IS OVER (2026-08-03).** It existed because *"a hand-interpreted checklist is not
 495: a merge licence"* â€” every green claim came from an agent transcribing its own local run. All
 496: three conditions that were set for ending it have landed: `at:verify --expect` (AI4DEV-25),
 497: the harness visible to tsc (AI4DEV-24), and CI on the PR head (AI4DEV-5) â€” now a **required**
 498: status check named `verify` on `main`, so a red run cannot be merged at all.
 499: 
 500: An item agent may therefore merge its own work, on one condition that is not negotiable: **the
 501: required CI check must be green on the exact head commit the merge decision pins.** Not a local
 502: run, not an earlier head. Verify the run id against the head SHA before merging, and record
 503: both. `strict` is false, so a branch need not be current with `main`; `enforce_admins` is false,
 504: so the founder can still merge past a red check deliberately. Neither is a licence for an agent
 505: to do so.
 506: 5. Post-merge check against merged `main`.
 507: 6. **Fold upward**: re-read the parent's children **fresh** (a sibling may have closed in a
 508:    parallel session); all Done or Cancelled â†’ parent folds; cascades upward. Cancelled counts
 509:    as closed but every cancelled child is **listed by name**, so a quietly-cancelled item can
 510:    never make a tree look complete. The cascade **stops below a requirement**.
 511: 
 512: ### When git and the board disagree (founder question, 2026-08-02)
 513: 
 514: Closing depends on an asynchronous integration: the merge fires a webhook and Linear moves the
 515: item. That transport can be slow, can drop, and can interleave with a sibling's merge.
 516: 
 517: **Git is the source of truth for merge state; Linear mirrors it.** So the rule is narrower than
 518: "never set Done by hand" â€” which, stated that way, forbade the repair without providing one and
 519: would strand an item In Progress forever. What is forbidden is **asserting a state that was
 520: never observed**. If the pull request is verifiably merged â€” the commit is on `main`, the API
 521: reports merged â€” then setting Done is not faking anything: the event happened and the evidence
 522: is in hand. Repair from primary evidence, and **record it as a repair**, never as the
 523: integration having worked.
 524: 
 525: | failure | handling |
 526: | --- | --- |
 527: | webhook slow | **bounded re-read (~30s)** before concluding anything. An instant check turns normal latency into a false alarm. |
 528: | webhook dropped | merge confirmed in git â†’ set Done, and say plainly the integration did not fire |
 529: | branch names no item | cannot auto-close, ever. Prevented upstream: the branch comes from Linear verbatim and is validated to tokenise 
to exactly this item |
 530: | **two siblings merging at once** | **confirm YOUR item is Done in Linear BEFORE reading the siblings.** That ordering is the whole 
fix: whoever finishes second necessarily observes the complete set, so the parent cannot be left unfolded by both sessions each seeing the 
other still open. Both folding is harmless â€” folding is idempotent. |
 531: | crash between merge and fold | nothing repairs it in the moment; the backstop is that `/work` on any parent re-reads and folds. Drift 
self-heals on next touch. |
 532: | Linear unreachable at merge | the merge still happened and the board is stale. Report it; the next `/work` reconciles. |
 533: 
 534: Deliberately absent: locks and transactions. For a handful of parallel sessions, converge-on-
 535: re-read plus reconcile-on-next-touch is proportionate, and the cost of being wrong is a board
 536: briefly behind â€” never lost work.
 537: 
 538: ### Requirement evidence gate â€” pinned, not asserted
 539: 
 540: 1. Exhaustive leaf snapshot at a named commit â€” every leaf listed with label and state.
 541: 2. Attribution resolved â€” refuse to close while the chain is unknown.
 542: 3. Acceptance suite green at integration tier at that commit, with named checks and timestamps.
 543: 4. Recorded founder attestation, with its date.
 544: 5. An explicit, recorded **waiver** path, so one flaky test cannot deadlock a requirement.
 545: 
 546: `/work` proposes. It never closes a requirement on its own.
 547: 
 548: ## Phase E â€” release
 549: 
 550: Clear the held item, print `session is free`, report the phase's still-open siblings with
 551: their labels, suggest the next `/work`. **Park** instead when stopping mid-item: commit WIP,
 552: release, item stays In Progress, resume later with `/work AI4DEV-19`.
 553: 
 554: ## Reflect on this skill â€” BEFORE the merge decision, not after
 555: 
 556: **Reflect while the pull request is still open.** The rule is that fixes found this way ride
 557: along in the item's own PR; scheduling the reflection after the merge made that impossible, and
 558: an item hit exactly that: by the time it was asked to reflect, its PR was closed and the only
 559: way to deliver four real findings was to open a second one, which the ride-along rule forbids.
 560: So the reflection belongs immediately **before** the merge decision, where its output can still
 561: be committed to the branch.
 562: 
 563: **If a finding arrives after the merge anyway** â€” the audit turns something up late, or the
 564: post-merge check does â€” do NOT open a second pull request for it. Report it to the coordinator,
 565: which folds it into the way of work directly. A finding with nowhere to go is a finding that
 566: gets dropped, and dropping it is worse than the small inconsistency of the coordinator carrying
 567: it.
 568: 
 569: Answer in plain sentences: **did `/work` behave as intended on this item, and does it need a
 570: fix?** Name what was awkward, what needed a rule that does not exist, and what a rule forced
 571: that turned out to be wrong.
 572: 
 573: Fixes found this way **ride along in the current item's PR**. This is the mechanism by which
 574: the way of work improves from use rather than from redesign sessions â€” and it is why the
 575: ride-along rule exists at all.
 576: 
 577: Two failures this exists to catch, both already observed:
 578: - A rule that cannot be followed (a step depending on a tool that is refused or unavailable)
 579:   and gets quietly skipped instead of fixed.
 580: - Ceremony out of proportion to the work, which is how a process stops being followed at all
 581:   rather than being followed badly.
 582: 
 583: ## Ride-along, and no nesting
 584: 
 585: **Ride-along. NEVER open a dev item for mechanics** (founder ruling 2026-08-02). Changes to the
 586: machinery made while working an item ride along in **that item's branch and PR** â€” no new item,
 587: no second PR, no exceptions for "it's small and clean". Listed in the PR body under "rides
 588: along", covered by the same gates because they are in the same diff.
 589: 
 590: Qualifies: what the item needs; small corrections to machinery being used while using it;
 591: anything the reflection step above turns up. Does not qualify: independent work that could
 592: stand alone and costs real time â€” **filed, not built**.
 593: 
 594: The failure this replaces: a one-line rule change became its own board item, its own worktree,
 595: its own branch and its own pull request in four minutes flat, bypassing every gate â€” while the
 596: session was in the middle of designing the process it bypassed.
 597: 
 598: **No nesting.** One session, one branch, one item. `/work` at something else while one is open
 599: refuses and offers three doors: finish, park, or file the new thing.
 600: 
 601: ## Never
 602: 
 603: - Never invent a branch name; never set an item Done by hand; never close a requirement
 604:   without the gate; never override the branch with a declared item; never silently pick between
 605:   disagreeing signals; never delete a leaf that has work against it; never let the stamp fail
 606:   quietly.
 607: - PowerShell, never Bash. bun, never npm/pnpm. Loop tier stays database-free.

