   1: # Expected-state declarations
   2: 
   3: One file per requirement â€” `req-016.json` here declares what
   4: `bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect` must find.
   5: 
   6: ## 1. What a declaration is
   7: 
   8: A declaration is the committed answer to "what is this requirement's suite supposed to report
   9: right now?" â€” which ids must be green, which must be red, and exactly why each red is red.
  10: 
  11: Without one, `at:verify` exits 1 whenever any id is red, and a requirement with honest reds waiting
  12: on later capability slices can never gate anything: a machine cannot tell "the eight declared green
  13: and the four declared reds" from "something broke". With one, it can.
  14: 
  15: `--expect` **refuses** rather than passes when the declaration cannot be honoured. All four of
  16: these exit 2, having run no tests at all:
  17: 
  18: - there is no declaration file for the requirement;
  19: - there is one, but it declares nothing for the tier being run;
  20: - it is malformed â€” bad JSON, an unknown tier key, a red that is not a well-formed declaration;
  21: - its ids are not in exact bijection with the acceptance file's P0 ids. A declaration that forgets
  22:   an id must never silently pass: that id would simply not be checked.
  23: 
  24: Without `--expect`, `at:verify` behaves exactly as it always has. The flag is additive.
  25: 
  26: ## 2. A red that turns green is a FAILURE
  27: 
  28: This surprises people, and it is deliberate. The declaration is the contract, so improving reality
  29: means updating the contract **in the same change**. Without that rule the gate cannot tell an
  30: improvement from a drift â€” and telling those apart is the entire point of the file.
  31: 
  32: So when a capability lands and an id goes green, the change that lands it moves the id from `red`
  33: to `green` here. That is one line in the same commit, and it is the record of what changed.
  34: 
  35: ## 3. How to write and update one
  36: 
  37: Run the suite and read what it actually reports:
  38: 
  39: ```
  40: bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop
  41: ```
  42: 
  43: Then copy the cause out of the reported detail into the declaration. A red is declared as an
  44: object carrying its **kind**, never as a free-text reason:
  45: 
  46: ```json
  47: "AT-016.11": { "kind": "capability-pending", "capabilities": ["H5 email provider simulator"] }
  48: ```
  49: 
  50: ```json
  51: "AT-016.05": { "kind": "pending", "phase": "sut-missing" }
  52: ```
  53: 
  54: **The match is exact, not a substring.** The first line the harness prints is rebuilt from what you
  55: declare and compared:
  56: 
  57: - `capability-pending` must match the **whole first line**. The line is
  58:   `CapabilityPending: CAPABILITY PENDING â€” ` followed by your capability names joined with `, ` in
  59:   the order you list them. So the names must be complete and in the harness's order: declaring
  60:   `H5 email provider` when the real detail says `H5 email provider simulator` fails, and so does
  61:   listing two capabilities the other way round.
  62: - `pending` must match `AtPending: <id> PENDING [<phase>] â€” ` as an **anchored prefix**. The class,
  63:   the id and the phase are all matched exactly, from the first character. Only the trailing detail
  64:   is free, because that tail is not something a declaration can determine: for `sut-missing` the
  65:   suite supplies its own words, and for `harness-missing` it embeds a module-resolution message
  66:   that differs by machine and checkout path. A declaration that could never be written on a second
  67:   machine would be a broken rule, not a stricter one.
  68: 
  69: Why exact rather than a substring: a substring cannot establish that a red has its declared
  70: **cause**. `H3 fault injection` as a free substring also matches
  71: `Error: H3 fault injection: fixture reset failed` â€” so a brand-new harness defect would satisfy a
  72: declaration that says "waiting on a capability", and the gate would pass. Matching the shape kills
  73: that: a fixture-reset failure is an `Error`, not a `CapabilityPending`, and cannot match.
  74: 
  75: **A red whose detail fits neither shape is undeclarable, and therefore fails.** That is deliberate:
  76: a red we cannot describe exactly is a red we do not understand, and it must not pass.
  77: 
  78: Two things a declaration may never contain, both refused when the file is read:
  79: 
  80: - **a comma inside a capability name.** Names are joined with `, `, so `["a, b"]` and `["a", "b"]`
  81:   produce the same line, and the declaration would stop saying which capabilities are pending;
  82: - **a redaction sentinel** such as `<redacted-token>`. That is what the runner prints *instead of*
  83:   something secret-shaped, so declaring one would match whatever had been redacted â€” a wildcard,
  84:   not a declaration. And if a real detail arrives redacted, the run fails closed with a message
  85:   saying the id is undeclarable until its detail no longer contains a secret-shaped token.
  86: 
  87: One consequence worth knowing: this couples declarations to the harness's message text. If that
  88: text changes, declarations break loudly with a diff naming the id â€” the correct failure direction.
  89: Machine-readable capability codes would decouple them and are filed as a follow-up.
  90: 
  91: ## 4. Changing a declaration is a governance act
  92: 
  93: No current-state checker can prove a red is **honest**. Nothing here can distinguish "this id is
  94: waiting on a capability that has not landed" from "someone broke this id and moved it to `red` with
  95: a matching reason". The declaration is an author's claim about intent, and the answer to that is
  96: review, not more machinery:
  97: 
  98: - treat a `green` â†’ `red` move with more suspicion than a code change;
  99: - a red that names a capability should point at the board item that will deliver it.
 100: 
 101: Making that reference mandatory and checking it is filed as a follow-up. Until then it is a
 102: reviewer's job.
 103: 
 104: ## 5. What `--expect` accounts for, and the one thing it still cannot see
 105: 
 106: Two layers of accounting run on every `--expect` pass, and both are needed.
 107: 
 108: **Test arithmetic.** Failing tests equal the declared reds, passing tests equal the declared
 109: greens, the total equals their sum, and nothing is skipped or todo. That closes the case where an
 110: extra failure no AT id claims â€” an untagged `it()` â€” hides behind an expected red.
 111: 
 112: **File accounting.** Every test *file* in the report is inspected too, because a failure can belong
 113: to a file and to no test at all: a file that fails to import, or a hook that throws. Two rules,
 114: both derived from what the reporter really emits: a file reported as failed must contain at least
 115: one failed test, and no file may carry a file-level error message. Without this a second file dying
 116: at import left every declared count untouched and the run exited 0.
 117: 
 118: **The one hole that remains, stated exactly** â€” this was measured against the installed reporter,
 119: not reasoned about. A hook that throws in a file that *also* contains a failing test is serialised
 120: identically to a healthy run of that file: same file status, same counts, same `success`. So while
 121: a red is declared in that same file, such a failure is invisible here. (A hook that throws in a
 122: file whose tests all pass IS caught, by the file accounting above.) Putting the raw vitest output
 123: in front of CI (AI4DEV-26) covers part of it; closing the rest needs a reporter-side envelope,
 124: which is filed.
 125: 
 126: Worth stating because it is easy to assume otherwise: on this axis `--expect` is **not** weaker
 127: than the plain no-flag gate. That gate's own process-exit rule only distinguishes a hook failure
 128: when no test failed at all, and `--expect` keeps exactly that rule for a declaration with no red.
 129: 
 130: ## 6. Two known limits
 131: 
 132: **Redaction.** The reported detail passes through the harness's redactor, which rewrites any run of
 133: 40 or more `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` characters to `<redacted-token>`. If a detail ever contained such a run,
 134: no declaration could reproduce the line, so that id is undeclarable: the run fails closed and says
 135: so, rather than matching something that identifies nothing. None of REQ-016's reds is affected â€”
 136: every detail contains spaces â€” but if you hit it, that is the cause.
 137: 
 138: **Integration-tier declarations are unexercised.** `--expect` is tier-agnostic by construction, but
 139: every conformance test runs at the loop tier, so the only integration-tier behaviour that has been
 140: exercised is the "no declaration for this tier" refusal. That changes when integration-tier
 141: declarations exist.

